  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2032}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{\u2032}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{\u2033}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,\u27f9\\,{P}^{\u2033}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{\u2033}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$",
